WASHINGTON — Congressman Jared Golden (ME-02) celebrated passage of the Take Care of America’s Veterans Act, legislation that strengthens veterans benefits and VA healthcare. The bill passed the House with bipartisan support in a voice vote.

“In return for their dedication and sacrifice, the country promises to take care of its veterans and their families. Policymakers must take pains to ensure we are upholding that promise,” Golden said. “This bill has many provisions, but the highlights are critical reforms to improve veterans' health care, including mental health care, and to better support wounded warriors and surviving spouses.

“Today is Veterans Day, when we recognize and honor the men and women who made a promise to protect and defend our country. I can think of no better way to honor them today than by supporting this bill.”

The Take Care of America’s Veterans Act includes the following bipartisan provisions, among others, many of which Congressman Golden has long supported:

The Major Richard Star Act, which ends the wounded-warriors tax and ensures veterans who retire after suffering a service-related injury receive both the retirement and disability benefits they earned through their service and dedication to their country;

The Veterans ACCESS Act, which increases access to residential treatment programs for veterans contending with substance use or mental health issues;

The Love Lives On Act, which ends the remarriage penalty so that surviving spouses of fallen service members can remarry without losing the benefits they need to care for their families;

The Sharri Briley and Eric Edmundson Veterans Benefits Expansion Act, which would require the VA to increase the base rate of monthly benefits for veterans with severe service-connected disabilities and survivor benefits for veterans who die from service-related injuries; and

Several provisions to expand GI Bill benefits, including increasing the monthly housing allowance for student veterans going to school online or apprenticeship training and expanding access to tech careers.

The Take Care of Veterans Act was supported by The American Legion, Wounded Warriors Project, American Veterans (AMVETS), Korean War Veterans Association, National Military Family Association, Veterans Justice Alliance and Vietnam Veterans of America, among others.

WASHINGTON — U.S. Senator Martin Heinrich (D-N.M.), a member of the U.S. Senate Appropriations Committee, and U.S. Senator Ben Ray Luján (D-N.M.), a member of the U.S. Senate Finance Subcommittee on Healthcare and one of two Head Start graduates to serve in the U.S. Senate, welcomed $11,538,279 from the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) to strengthen Head Start programs for New Mexico families, expand maternal and child health services, and research interventions to support individuals affected by Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders (FASD).

“Every kid deserves a strong start in life, and parents should have the support they need to give their children that opportunity,” said Heinrich, a member of the U.S. Senate Appropriations Committee. “This $11.6 million will help more New Mexico families access early childhood education and the healthcare they need to raise healthy kids. It will also support research at the University of New Mexico to find better ways to care for families affected by Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders.”

“I’m proud to have helped secure more than $11 million in investments for New Mexico’s education and health care,” said Luján. “As one of only two senators who attended Head Start, I know how much Head Start programs mean to our children and families. I’ll keep fighting for investments that prepare New Mexico students for the future and expand access to health care.”

The full list of HHS grants for New Mexico is below:

$635,765 for the Alamo Navajo School Board’s Head Start program;

$434,773 for the Ramah Navajo School Board’s Head Start program;

$1,048,042 for New Mexico State University’s Head Start program;

$8,528,164 for Presbyterian Medical Services’ Head Start program;

$852,226 for maternal and child health services through the New Mexico Department of Health; and

$39,309 to support the New Mexico Alcohol Research Center at the University of New Mexico, which is dedicated to researching the neurological mechanisms and behavioral challenges associated with Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders.

Nearly 43 million Americans hold $1.6 trillion in cumulative student loan debt

WASHINGTON – U.S. Senate Democratic Whip Dick Durbin (D-IL) today led 11 Senators in reintroducing legislation to ensure struggling student loan borrowers are treated fairly and understand the full range of repayment options and resources available to them. The Student Loan Borrower Bill of Rights creates consistent servicing and disclosure standards across private and federal student loans and allows the Secretary of Education to adjust those standards for federal servicers through regulation to benefit federal student loan borrowers.

“No American should have to spend decades paying off student loan debt. The rising cost of higher education has placed an enormous financial burden on both students and their families, forcing many young people to delay buying a home, starting a family, or pursuing other important milestones,” Durbin said. “The Student Loan Borrower Bill of Rights sets out basic rights that I believe all student borrowers should be entitled to, so that student debt does not stand in the way of the next generation’s future.”

“This bill would ensure that student loan borrowers do not bear the brunt of the financial consequences that can come from student loan servicing errors, while improving servicing quality and bringing much-needed transparency and protections to the private student loan market,” said Sara Partridge, Associate Director of Higher Education at Center for American Progress.

“Student loan borrowers deserve clarity, respect, and reliable support as they navigate repayment. The Student Loan Borrower Bill of Rights takes important steps to strengthen protections for federal and private student loans, hold servicers accountable, and ensure borrowers receive timely and accurate information to help them successfully manage their payments. Third Way is grateful for Senator Durbin’s leadership to make the student loan system work better for students,” said Michelle Dimino, Director of Education at Third Way.

“Student loan borrowers are facing an onslaught of harm and confusion as a result of the Trump Administration’s nonstop chaos. From skyrocketing bills, to servicing errors and misinformation that violate their rights, it is long past time for an overhaul. We applaud Senator Durbin for introducing the Student Loan Borrower Bill of Rights and look forward to making sure this important bill becomes law,” said Aissa Canchola Bañez, Policy Director for Protect Borrowers.

Student loan debt is second only to mortgages as one of the largest sources of consumer debt. In 2025, approximately 43 million borrowers held $1.6 trillion in student loan debt. This crushing debt prevents millions of Americans from fully participating in our nation’s economy by reducing homeownership, jeopardizing retirement security, and limiting entrepreneurship.

Repaying federal and private student loans can be complex and confusing. Borrowers often face increased costs because they are unable to obtain alternative repayment options, resolve errors in a timely manner, apply payments to reduce the overall cost of the loan, and obtain accurate information about their eligibility for benefits. The already complicated student loan system has become even more difficult to navigate after Republicans passed the One Big Beautiful Bill Act, which eliminated the most consumer-friendly repayment plans. Further, with the post-pandemic return to repayment, many borrowers have received incorrect or late billing statements from their servicers and have encountered long wait times to receive help.

Specifically, the Student Loan Borrower Bill of Rights would:

Improve disclosures and outreach to borrowers when their loan is sold or transferred, the borrower is identified as at-risk of default or having trouble making payments, or the borrower is eligible for alternative repayment options or other benefits;

Standardize the application and allocation of payments among multiple loans in a manner most beneficial to the borrower;

Create a Student Loan Servicing Interagency Working Group to provide recommendations related to servicing contracts;

Remove a borrower’s record of default from their credit history when they act to rectify their defaulted loan;

Limit circumstances in which borrowers are subject to late fees and other negative consequences;

Improve timely resolution of errors made by servicers;

Prohibit servicers from using mandatory arbitration clauses, class action restrictions, and immunity defenses to prevent borrowers from suing their servicer in a court of law;

Require servicers to establish Repayment Specialists, which are specially trained units focused on keeping at-risk borrowers from defaulting, without pushing them into forbearance, and getting them back on track to repayment;

Improve private loan protections, including ensuring private student loans are discharged upon death or disability of the borrower;

Require institutions of higher education to certify private student loans prior to a borrower taking out a private loan to ensure that federal loan eligibility is exhausted;

Establish accountability processes for servicers and lenders to ensure they are adequately serving borrowers;

Codify the authority of the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau Student Loan Ombudsman; and

Codify a Student Loan Forgiveness Payment Counter, allowing borrowers to see their payment history and the number of qualifying payments they have made toward their loan forgiveness program.

Cosponsoring this bill are U.S. Senators Jack Reed (D-RI), Elizabeth Warren (D-MA), Amy Klobuchar (D-MN), Tina Smith (D-MN), Richard Blumenthal (D-CT), Ben Ray Luján (D-NM), Jeff Merkley (D-OR), Tammy Duckworth (D-IL), Martin Heinrich (D-NM), Jeanne Shaheen (D-NH), and Ron Wyden (D-OR).

The bill has earned endorsements from Protect Borrowers, Third Way, Center for American Progress, Center for Responsible Lender, National Consumer Law Center (on behalf of its low-income clients), EdTrust, Student Debt Crisis Center, National Association of Consumer Advocates, Consumer Action, National Consumers League, Americans for Financial Reform, Consumer Federation of America, TICAS, New America Higher Education Program, and UnidosUS.

